  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\n9 PM Update...Have expanded the watch to cover the entire nrn\nhalf of ENC, as good dynamics in place here with the low moving\nacrs the Delmarva. Latest SCP indicative of values above 15 in\nthis area should allow for progression of svr threat all the way\nto the OBX zones later this evening. Further south, uncertainty\nin storm covg as little convective develpment attm, possibly\ntied to some lack of moisture and developing nocturnal CIN.\nFurther updates to the watch area are still possible and will\ncont to monitor. \n\nAn anomalously strong upper level trough\namplifies over the eastern US today, with a potent shortwave \ndiving southeast through VA and the Carolinas. A strong LLJ \nahead of this feature should lead to increasing moisture \nadvection, with PWATs once again climbing back above 2\". \nMeanwhile, heating of a moist boundary layer should support \nmoderate instability, with MLCAPE rising into the 1500-2500 j/kg\nrange. MHX 18Z sounding shows MUCAPE in excess of 5kJ/kg, but\nwe've been relatively clear from the debris clouds . Aloft, a \nnotable upper jet max is forecast to overspread the instability \naxis, supporting deep layer shear of 30-45kt. While instability \nwill be moderate, the shear/instability/forcing combo is notable\nfor this time of year. This combination supports the threat for\nsevere weather, with modest support from ensemble, analog, and \nmachine learning guidance. The main potential limiting factors \nare timing and potential subsidence, with area remaining capped \nthrough much of today. Most guidance continues to show that the\nstronger convection will not reach ENC until, or after, sunset.\nDespite the potential for a gradually stabilizing boundary \nlayer, if strong large-scale forcing is realized, it should help\nsupport deep convection well past the typical diurnal max in \nconvective intensity this time of year. Thus the reason the\nwatch is in effect until 1am. The watch may need to be extended\neastward through portions of ENC later this evening, as TS probs\nfor the OBX zones have increased a bit. Will see how upstream\ndevelopment occurs in VA before any watch extention attm. Storm\nmode could be a bit messy, with combination of supercells early\nahead of more organized multicell line segments later. Given \nthe potential amount of forcing present, it appears likely that \na MCS will move through the eastern Carolinas and VA during the \nevening and early overnight hours posing a risk of damaging \nwinds. Low- level SRH around 100-200m2/s2 may support a few \nrotating updrafts capable of hail and tornados. However, those \nlatter 2 hazards should be lower compared to the wind risk. \nGiven the trend in the track of the low further away from the FA\nand the later arrival of convection, SPC has removed the FA \nfrom the 5% tor threat, limiting the potential to 2% across all \nof ENC. While convection looks to be fairly progressive, heavy \nrain in a high PWAT airmass could pose a localized flood threat,\nesp for areas that have received heavy rain over the last few \ndays (Hyde county east through the Outer Banks), where WPC is \nsuggesting 6hr rainfall amounts in excess of one inch.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cold front will push offshore early Wed \nmorning, with sfc high pressure building in from the west while \ntroughing continues aloft. This will keep temps seasonable and \nmostly dry through late week. The next frontal system will \nimpact the area this weekend into early next week, with \nincreasing precip chances.",
